    "question": "Which statement correctly describes: When silicon dioxide (SiO2) is treated with aqueous hydrofluoric acid (HF), the compound formed is:",
    "options": {
      "A": "H2SiF6 (hydrofluorosilicic acid) and SiF4",
      "B": "SiH4 and OF2",
      "C": "SiO and F2",
      "D": "SiF2 and H2O"
    },
    "optionsList": [
      "H2SiF6 (hydrofluorosilicic acid) and SiF4",
      "SiH4 and OF2",
      "SiO and F2",
      "SiF2 and H2O"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nChemical inertness of quartz and reaction with HF.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nSiO2 is insoluble in all common acids but dissolves in HF.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nSiO2 + 4HF -> SiF4 + 2H2O; SiF4 + 2HF -> H2SiF6.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Silica is extremely inert due to strong 3D Si-O bonds.\n2. However, it reacts readily with hydrofluoric acid because of the exceptionally high Si-F bond energy.\n3. It first forms silicon tetrafluoride (SiF4), which dissolves in excess HF to yield hydrofluorosilicic acid (H2SiF6).\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: H2SiF6 (hydrofluorosilicic acid) and SiF4\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nGlass etching relies on this unique reaction of silica with HF.",
    "solutionStructured": {
      "concept": "Chemical inertness of quartz and reaction with HF.",
      "given": "SiO2 is insoluble in all common acids but dissolves in HF.",
      "formula": "SiO2 + 4HF -> SiF4 + 2H2O; SiF4 + 2HF -> H2SiF6.",
      "stepByStep": "1. Silica is extremely inert due to strong 3D Si-O bonds.\n2. However, it reacts readily with hydrofluoric acid because of the exceptionally high Si-F bond energy.\n3. It first forms silicon tetrafluoride (SiF4), which dissolves in excess HF to yield hydrofluorosilicic acid (H2SiF6).",
      "finalAnswer": "Option A: H2SiF6 (hydrofluorosilicic acid) and SiF4",
      "whyOtherOptionsWrong": "Glass etching relies on this unique reaction of silica with HF."
    },
